anyboot: compute MBR partition extents in 64 bit

src/tools/anyboot/anyboot.cpp's createPartition() took its byte offset and
byte size as uint32_t, but main() passes off_t values taken straight from
fstat().  Any anyboot image whose BFS partition is 4 GiB or larger therefore
had its extents silently truncated modulo 2^32 before they were divided down
to sectors and written into the MBR.

Observed on a 10 GiB builder image (HAIKU_IMAGE_SIZE = 10240):

  imageSize   = 10737418240 bytes
  (uint32_t)    10737418240 % 2^32 = 2147483648
  / 512       = 4194304 sectors  <- what landed in MBR entry 1
  correct     = 20971520 sectors

Entry 2 (the EFI FAT partition) is passed the 64 bit efiOffset, so its data
is written at the right place in the file, but the entry recorded the
truncated offset and so pointed 8 GiB too early -- inside the BFS.  The
resulting table describes a 2 GiB "BFS" overlapped by an "EFI" partition
that is really BFS content, which is fatal both to firmware and to
`losetup -P` (used to inject the SSH key at build time).

The MBR format itself is not the limit: its LBA fields are 32 bit *sector*
counts, i.e. 2 TiB at 512 bytes/sector.  Only the tool was wrong.

This widens createPartition()'s offset/size to uint64_t, does the
byte->sector division in 64 bit, and narrows to uint32_t only when filling
the on-disk fields -- behind an explicit check that errors out instead of
wrapping again if a partition ever really does exceed the MBR's 2 TiB reach.
The same widening is applied coherently to every arithmetic site involved:
the CHS helper chsAddressFor() (which takes a sector number), the bootable
back-patch pwrite() offset, the TRACE format specifiers (%u against a 64 bit
value is undefined behaviour and printed garbage), and both call sites in
main().  The partition entry fields are also filled via memcpy() rather than
through a misaligned uint32_t* cast over a uint8_t array.

